    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Libertarianism is fundamentally rooted in the Lockean concept of self-ownership, which dictates that an individual has absolute dominion over their own body and justly acquired property, making involuntary taxation a violation of natural rights. Social democracy draws heavily from Rawlsian distributive justice, arguing that a just society must structure its institutions to benefit the least advantaged, thereby necessitating progressive taxation. The libertarian view sees the state as a necessary evil whose power must be strictly limited to protect these pre-political rights. In contrast, the social democratic view sees the state as the primary mechanism through which true freedom—freedom *from* want—can be achieved for everyone.",
      "source": "target",
